(c) The list bounds 4 j_H: ripe (j_H = 1), hedge with d decisions (j_H = 1 + d), i-th post-mortem
    entry (j_H = 1 + d + i). Single mistake late in the run, so S and U rarely help.
    j_H   sched   max queries   mean   Pr[queries > 4 j_H]   (4 j_H)
     1  SUSH            4     3.97       0.0000          (4)
     1  rand           41     3.92       0.3084          (4)
     4  SUSH           16    15.55       0.0000          (16)
     4  rand           60    15.45       0.3826          (16)
     9  SUSH           36    33.59       0.0000          (36)
     9  rand           91    33.30       0.3744          (36)

(d) Lemma 3's proof: 'stream i is served at least once in every ceil(1/w_i) queries, up to rounding,
    so a list stream reaches its first mistake within about j_i/w_i queries.' For weights that are not
    reciprocals of integers, 'once in every ceil(1/w_i)' gives j*ceil(1/w_i), not j/w_i + O(1).
    A proportional (largest-deficit) schedule for w = (0.4, 0.3, 0.3):
    stream S: w = 0.4, max over j of (position of j-th service - j/w) = 2.50  (<= 1/w = 2.50); largest gap 3 vs ceil(1/w) = 3
    stream U: w = 0.3, max over j of (position of j-th service - j/w) = 2.00  (<= 1/w = 3.33); largest gap 4 vs ceil(1/w) = 4
    stream H: w = 0.3, max over j of (position of j-th service - j/w) = 3.00  (<= 1/w = 3.33); largest gap 4 vs ceil(1/w) = 4
    -> here the gaps stay within ceil(1/w_i), but that premise only yields j*ceil(1/w_i) (3j for w = 0.4,
       not 2.5j), so the proof's inference does not follow. The conclusion is still true: a proportional
       schedule has bounded discrepancy, and the j-th service comes by position (j+1)/w_i (excess <= 1/w_i).
       For the default weights (1/2, 1/4, 1/4) the schedule S,U,S,H has gaps exactly 2, 4, 4 and no rounding.

====================================================================================================
Part 2(a). Theorem 5(b) with final-answer asks only: a two-step product class
====================================================================================================
Problems p = 1..m. Step 0 writes a choice in {a, b}; step 1 writes the claimed solution 'claim'
(the final-answer slot). The human writes a; hypotheses write a or b at step 0 independently per
problem with prior Pr[a] = 0.4; all write 'claim' at step 1. V accepts iff step 0 is a (S_V is
preserved by appending; (A2), (A3') hold). Exact Bayes by enumeration over the 2^m hypotheses.
  m =  1, final-answer asks only      : K =   1.32, rejections =  1, asks =  0, K/b_alpha =  0.40, K/log2(1/(1-alpha)) =   8.70, min hit value = 1.32 bits vs b_alpha = 3.32   <-- VIOLATES 5(b)
  m =  1, asks at every step (SI-ABC) : K =   1.32, rejections =  0, asks =  1, K/b_alpha =  0.40, K/log2(1/(1-alpha)) =   8.70, min hit value = inf bits vs b_alpha = 3.32
  m =  4, final-answer asks only      : K =   5.29, rejections =  4, asks =  0, K/b_alpha =  1.59, K/log2(1/(1-alpha)) =  34.79, min hit value = 1.32 bits vs b_alpha = 3.32   <-- VIOLATES 5(b)
  m =  4, asks at every step (SI-ABC) : K =   5.29, rejections =  0, asks =  4, K/b_alpha =  1.59, K/log2(1/(1-alpha)) =  34.79, min hit value = inf bits vs b_alpha = 3.32
  m = 10, final-answer asks only      : K =  13.22, rejections = 10, asks =  0, K/b_alpha =  3.98, K/log2(1/(1-alpha)) =  86.97, min hit value = 1.32 bits vs b_alpha = 3.32   <-- VIOLATES 5(b)
  m = 10, asks at every step (SI-ABC) : K =  13.22, rejections =  0, asks = 10, K/b_alpha =  3.98, K/log2(1/(1-alpha)) =  86.97, min hit value = inf bits vs b_alpha = 3.32
  -> With asks restricted to the final-answer slot, a hit at a committed step elsewhere is worth only
     log2(1/0.4) = 1.32 bits, not log2(1/alpha) = 3.32. The count K/b_alpha of Theorem 5(b) and the
     K/b_alpha factors of 5(e) and 5(f) are false; K (one bit per hit, Lemma 1 of note.md) is right.

====================================================================================================
Part 2(b). Exact-Bayes toy VSI (referee 6's class, SUBSEQ humans, D = 0), default weights
====================================================================================================
n0 = 4, sigma = 3, 4 problems, 30 rounds per path, H = adversarial list, 100 paths per row.
Asks either at every step (SI-ABC) or only at the claim-writing slot (section 2.2's option).
Columns: worst over paths of (rejections + audit hits)/(K/b_alpha); share of hits worth < b_alpha;
smallest hit value / b_alpha; worst search length vs 2 n0; searches over 2 n0; rejected runs with
no committed mistake below n0 (Lemma 1).
  asks        alpha  sched  max (rej+audit)/(K/b)  low-value hits  min bits/b  max search  >2n0  L1-fail
  every step   0.50  SUSH                   0.34      0/197          1.00          7      0      0
  every step   0.50  rand                   0.34      0/200          1.00         11      2      0
  every step   0.30  SUSH                   0.35      0/70           1.46          5      0      0
  every step   0.30  rand                   0.35      0/72           1.46          6      0      0
  every step   0.15  SUSH                   0.00      0/0             inf          0      0      0
  every step   0.15  rand                   0.00      0/0             inf          0      0      0
  final slot   0.50  SUSH                   0.34      0/203          1.00          7      0      0
  final slot   0.50  rand                   0.34      0/208          1.00         11      2      0
  final slot   0.30  SUSH                   0.58     33/92           0.58          7      0      0
  final slot   0.30  rand                   0.58     35/94           0.58          6      0      0
  final slot   0.15  SUSH                   0.74     75/92           0.37          3      0      0
  final slot   0.15  rand                   0.74     75/91           0.37          4      0      0
  total rejected runs over all rows: 919 (Lemma 1 checked on each)
  -> with asks at every step every hit is worth >= b_alpha. With asks at the claim slot only, 36-82%
     of hits are worth less than b_alpha (down to 0.37 b_alpha, i.e. about 1 bit), so the per-hit step
     of Theorem 5(b)'s proof fails; in this small class the total stays below K/b_alpha because K is
     loose here (Part 2(a) shows the total itself exceeding K/b_alpha).
     S,U,S,H never exceeds 2 n0 = 8 queries; the random schedule does (11).

====================================================================================================
Part 3. Theorem 5(b)-(c) use (M1), (M2) for the 'nothing bizarre' check; without (M2) VSI loops
====================================================================================================
Class {P} (K = 0). P writes x0 -> 'u' -> 'v' -> 'z' -> ... ; V accepts (S_V holds). The bizarre check
flags any notebook containing 'z', which P itself writes at step 2: (M2) fails.
  after 1000 reruns: 1000 task queries, no hit, no output, no abstention -> does not terminate.
  Fix: assume (M1), (M2) (as slack-imitation.md does for its check), or, as note.md Proposition 9
  does, output the verified artifact marked 'flagged where the human would be' when the flip query
  shows no mistake.

====================================================================================================
Part 4. Theorem 5(f) needs S* to be reached by the human's own chain (tau*), not only S_V
====================================================================================================
Class {P} (K = 0). V accepts P's chain (S_V holds, (A2), (A3') hold) but the chain fails S*:
(A4') fails on the human's own notebook. Every run is P's chain, has no mistake, and is output.
  T =    10: failed outputs =    10;  claimed bound K/(b_alpha beta gamma) = 0
  T =   100: failed outputs =   100;  claimed bound K/(b_alpha beta gamma) = 0
  T =  1000: failed outputs =  1000;  claimed bound K/(b_alpha beta gamma) = 0
  -> Lemma S must be applied to tau* (remaining time to S*): (f) needs S* preserved by appending,
     tau*(x0) <= n0, and (B_D) measured in tau*. None of these is assumed.

====================================================================================================
Part 5. Section 6: '(A2) for every problem ... with n0 at the 75th percentile, 6-11% are not finished'
====================================================================================================
Thread S Part E computes phi = Pr[tau > n] with n = 2 n0. With n0 at the q-quantile, (A2)
(tau <= n0) fails on 1 - q of problems; only those with tau > n make VSI abstain.
   q     law                Pr[tau > n0] (A2 fails)   Pr[n0 < tau <= 2n0]   Pr[tau > 2n0] (abstain)
  0.75   lognormal s=0.75        0.250                    0.195                0.055
  0.75   lognormal s=1.00        0.250                    0.164                0.086
  0.75   lognormal s=1.25        0.250                    0.140                0.110
  0.75   Pareto a=1.2             0.250                    0.141                0.109
  0.75   Pareto a=1.5             0.250                    0.162                0.088
  0.75   Pareto a=2.0             0.250                    0.188                0.062
  -> the 6-11% are abstentions; (A2) itself fails on 25%. On the 14-19% with n0 < tau <= 2n0, Lemma 1
     fails (the first mistake may lie anywhere before n), so each of their rejections may cost up to
     2n = 4 n0 under S,U,S,H, not 2 n0, and Lemma S guarantees fewer mistakes.

====================================================================================================
Part 6. Section 7 'worst-case effort': a factor 2 margin, before audits and abstentions
====================================================================================================
Budget: a fifth of chained imitation's guarantee at eps = 5%, 4 n0 K. VSI: 2 iota n0 K (search, default
weights) + beta T (audits; beta = 1 recommended with human verifiers) + c_V (T + K) (verifications,
c_V task steps each) + phi T 2n (abstentions, n = 2 iota n0). Largest T/K meeting the budget, n0 = 30:
  iota  c_V  beta |  phi=0    phi=0.05  phi=0.10
   1.0  0.0   0.0 |      inf      10.0       5.0
   1.0  0.0   1.0 |     60.0       8.6       4.6
   1.0  0.1   0.0 |    599.0       9.8       5.0
   1.0  0.1   1.0 |     54.5       8.4       4.6
   1.0  0.5   0.0 |    119.0       9.2       4.8
   1.0  0.5   1.0 |     39.7       7.9       4.4
   1.3  0.0   0.0 |      inf       5.4       2.7
   1.3  0.0   1.0 |     42.0       4.8       2.5
   1.3  0.1   0.0 |    419.0       5.3       2.7
   1.3  0.1   1.0 |     38.1       4.7       2.5
   1.3  0.5   0.0 |     83.0       5.0       2.6
   1.3  0.5   1.0 |     27.7       4.5       2.4
   1.6  0.0   0.0 |      inf       2.5       1.2
   1.6  0.0   1.0 |     24.0       2.3       1.2
   1.6  0.1   0.0 |    239.0       2.5       1.2
   1.6  0.1   1.0 |     21.7       2.2       1.2
   1.6  0.5   0.0 |     47.0       2.3       1.2
   1.6  0.5   1.0 |     15.7       2.1       1.1
  -> under the conditioning (phi = 0) with a machine verifier (c_V = 0, beta = 0) there is no limit;
     with a human verifier and beta = 1 the bar holds only for T up to about 30-55 K; with heavy tails
     (phi = 5-10%, outside the conditioning) only up to about 2-8 K. The human-does-it baseline needs T > 2K.

